  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "note-overview",
      "type": "text_bubble",
      "position": {
        "x": 80,
        "y": 440
      },
      "width": 440,
      "height": 390,
      "style": {
        "width": 440,
        "height": 390
      },
      "data": {
        "label": "Note",
        "title": "MULTI-PSV & RUPTURE DISC PROTECTION",
        "text": "A volumetric pump (120 L/min) feeds a process line through a 4 mm orifice that cannot pass the full flow, so the pressure rises until the relief devices act.\n\nPSV-101 (conventional, pop action, set 19.0 barg, 25 % blowdown) pops and stays at full lift: its inlet settles at about 15.7 barg, above the 14.25 barg reseat pressure, and it relieves about 92 L/min. Switch it to Modulating to see it hold about 20.9 barg at 83 % lift, or lower the blowdown to 7 %: full lift would then drop the inlet below reseat, so the valve cycles between reseat and set and the average relief load is shown. Pop action latching stays open whatever the blowdown.\n\nPSV-102 (pilot-operated, modulating, 24.0 barg) senses the pump discharge through its pilot line; the rupture disc RD-101 (29.0 barg) is the last stage. Solve all cases to follow the relief cascade: blocking PSV-101 lifts PSV-102, blocking both bursts the rupture disc. The Relief Analysis Matrix in the data panel lists opening order and backpressure. All lines run below 1 m/s.\n\nInstruments: PIT 101 pump discharge pressure (H below the PSV-101 set point, HH below PSV-102), FIT 102 relief flow and FIT 103 process flow.",
        "fontSize": "md",
        "rotation": 0
      }
    },
